---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SOURCE_LIBRARY, tools } from '../data/tools';

const sources = Object.values(SOURCE_LIBRARY);
---

<BaseLayout title="Calculation & Editorial Standards" description="How Dama Workshop verifies woodworking calculations, handles rounding, documents limitations and corrects errors." activeNav="/standards/">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell page-hero__grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Calculation standards</p><h1>Results you can reproduce.</h1></div>
      <p>Every published result is tied to a visible formula, an explicit unit convention and a repeatable test. This page explains what “verified” means—and what it does not mean.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--paper">
    <div class="shell about-grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Verification pipeline</p><h2>Four checks before publication.</h2></div>
      <ol class="standards-steps">
        <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Define the relationship</strong><p>We write the formula and name each assumption before implementing it. A calculator cannot silently decide hardware clearance, waste allowance or rounding precision.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Test known examples</strong><p>Automated checks cover default examples, fractional inputs, boundary values and invalid physical dimensions. The production build fails when a required page, reference block or local link is missing.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Compare explanation and output</strong><p>The method, worked example and calculator must use the same convention. When a result is an estimate or heuristic, the page says so.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>04</span><div><strong>Publish the verification record</strong><p>Each tool displays its version and last verification date. Material changes are recorded in the public verification log.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell standards-grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Precision</p><h2>Units, fractions and rounding.</h2></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>One international inch is exactly 25.4 millimetres. Decimal-to-fraction results are rounded to the denominator selected by the user, then reduced. The displayed rounding difference makes that approximation visible.</p>
        <p>Fraction inputs accept whole numbers, decimals, simple fractions and mixed numbers. Denominators of zero, non-finite values and physically impossible dimensions return an error instead of a plausible-looking result.</p>
        <p>Displayed values may be rounded for readability. They are planning values, not evidence that stock, machinery or an opening is dimensionally exact.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="shell standards-grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Scope limits</p><h2>A calculator is not a shop inspection.</h2></div>
      <ul class="check-list check-list--light">
        <li>Measure actual stock, openings, blades and hardware before cutting.</li>
        <li>Follow the current manufacturer manual for product-specific clearances.</li>
        <li>Inspect grain, defects and machine setup; software cannot see them.</li>
        <li>Use appropriate guards, dust controls and personal protective equipment.</li>
        <li>Treat cutting layouts as planning aids and confirm every mark on the workpiece.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--paper">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">Reference foundation</p><h2>Primary sources we use.</h2></div><p>Official standards, government technical guidance and manufacturer documentation take priority over anonymous summaries.</p></div>
      <ol class="source-list source-list--grid">{sources.map((source) => <li><a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>{source.title}</strong><span>{source.note}</span></a></li>)}</ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell about-grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Current coverage</p><h2>{tools.length} calculators under one standard.</h2></div>
      <div class="prose"><p>Verification reduces avoidable arithmetic and interface errors; it does not convert general planning guidance into engineering advice. If you find a mismatch between a formula, example and output, report the exact input and result.</p><p><a class="button button--primary" href="/contact/">Report a correction</a> <a class="text-link" href="/changelog/">Read the verification log →</a></p></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
